  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14190876/assets/c11e098426544491bf77324dc380997c5883eff6ea5b589d75bd80055ecc3d83.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$",
  "worked_solution": "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary point was identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1.5)$ | $-1.5$ | $(-1.5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n\nAdditionally, from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points.  Also, the graph of $y=f(x)$ is concave down in $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ and $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14190876/assets/9f9206d1c2aaa0bd07ad5009b71cd8d67dca142455d04c465b4ece62fde040a2.png)",
